>
> On 6 January 2011 03:17, Jonathan Jones <eyeonus@gmail.com> wrote:
>>
>> Waiter: Do you want cream or sugar in your coffee?
>>
>> Lojbanist: Yes.
>
> Proper response is "I do." :D Unless you drink your coffee with neither..=
.

If you want your coffee with neither, the correct answer is "no". "I do" an=
d=20
"yes" are merely semantically different - the meaning is the same in both=
=20
responses.
=20
>
> Also, that would depend on the waiter's emphasis of the sentence:
>
> "Do you want CREAM or SUGAR in your coffee?"
>
> means:
>
> "Is this true: (You want cream in your coffee. =E2=8A=95 You want sugar i=
n your=20
>coffee.)?"
>
> "Do you WANT cream or sugar in your coffee?"
>
> means:
>
> "Is this true: (You want cream in your coffee. =E2=88=A8 You want sugar i=
n your=20
>coffee.)?"
>
> ("=E2=8A=95" is XOR, "=E2=88=A8" is OR.)
>
> So presuming the waiter emphasised "cream" and "sugar", not "want", "yes"=
 or "I=20
>do" are not proper responses.=20

=20
It doesn't matter what the waiter emphasized.

Let us assign the following:
p =3D wants cream
q =3D wants sugar

(Taken directly from=20
http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Truth_table#Logical_disjunction):
The truth table for p OR q (also written as p =E2=88=A8 q, p || q, or p + q=
) is as=20
follows:=20



=20
p
q
p =E2=88=A8 q
T T T=20
T F T=20
F T T=20
F F F L1 (wants cream, but not sugar): Yes.
L2 (wants sugar, but not cream): Yes.
L3 (wants both cream and sugar): Yes.
L4 (wants neither): No.

To translate the waiter's sentence into Lojban:

W: xu do djica lo nu lo kruji .a lo sakta cu nenri lo ckafi

Lojban {.a}, by the way, is Logical OR. XOR is {.onai}.

